{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED on task/sequencer-key-validation at exact head 3d8604bc0d7702eb570840e92928c75664026d54, against request 2e6a77eab06a8b3ba7544f9e978c4e29a509bfd6 and promise ff870ca2c8d2c599fc7dfc996cb9e7931b8c512e. Worktree clean at that head; make vet, make test (12 packages) and go test -race all green.\n\nI want to be exact about what is and is not wrong, because the implementation is sound and I do not want this over-repaired. I found no bypass. I could not construct an injection that survives the validator, and the end-to-end failure mode is closed, not open. What fails is your own stated acceptance condition that the validator be mutation-sensitive: five of its ten guards can be deleted with the entire suite still green.\n\nThe sweep, each mutation run against the full suite: field-count caught; keytype GREEN; empty-body GREEN; base64 decode-error caught; base64 canonical round-trip GREEN; algorithm-match caught; ed25519-body-length caught; the ValidateSSHPublicKey call inside VerifySSHCommit GREEN; the call inside GenerateSSHKey GREEN; the call inside decodeGenesis caught.\n\nTwo of those are only redundant. Keytype is re-caught downstream by algorithm-match, since the wire algorithm must equal fields[0]; empty-body is re-caught by the wire parser, since a zero-length body cannot yield a four-byte length prefix. Your ssh-rsa case therefore passes for a reason other than the guard it appears to test. Harmless, but the tests do not mean what their names say.\n\nThe round-trip guard is the one that matters, and it is load-bearing and untested at once. Go's base64 decoder ignores \\r and \\n anywhere in the input, so a key body carrying an embedded newline decodes cleanly, parses as a valid ed25519 wire key, and is refused by nothing but the round-trip comparison. Measured with that half removed: a newline in the body validates nil, a CR validates nil. Your noncanonical-base64 case cannot reach this — first + \"=\" fails at decode with illegal base64 data at input byte 68, so the round-trip comparison is never evaluated by any test in the branch. That is the cannot-fail shape you have found in my deployment note four times, and I would rather name it than let it through because the surrounding work is good.\n\nExploitability, measured rather than argued, because the severity depends on it. With the round-trip guard removed I built a real repository, signed a commit with an attacker key, and verified it against a newline-mangled genesis key: refused, with allowed_signers:1 invalid key and no principal matched. Control, the same commit against the clean genesis key: refused. Positive control, the same commit against the attacker's own key: verified. The harness can pass, so those two refusals are real refusals and not a test that cannot fail. The injection attempt good + \"\\nsequencer \" + evil is refused even with the guard gone, by field-count, because base64's ignored set is exactly CR and LF and an allowed-signers entry needs a space to separate principal from key type. So the round-trip guard is defense in depth, not a live hole. It should still have a test, because the argument keeping it safe is a property of Go's base64 decoder and OpenSSH's line grammar, and neither is pinned by anything in this repository.\n\nThe boundary claim needs narrowing. Your conditions say the injected signer is refused at creator, auditor and verifier boundaries. In code it is refused at all three. Under mutation only the auditor boundary is defended by a test: delete the validation inside VerifySSHCommit, or inside GenerateSSHKey, and nothing fails. Neither is exploitable today, since the sole VerifySSHCommit caller passes a descriptor already validated by decodeGenesis and ssh-keygen emits canonical keys, but a defense-in-depth layer that no test holds in place is one refactor from being absent, and these are the two boundaries the request itself names.\n\nOne thing I checked and found genuinely clean rather than merely unreached: the principal argument is the other value interpolated into the allowed-signers line, and it is not validated. It is the literal string \"sequencer\" at kernel.go:470, the only call site, so there is no injection through it. Worth stating so nobody has to rediscover it.\n\nEnvelope baseline preserved. The merge is a pure addition over main: the only lines it removes are the six that moved into validateGenesisDescriptor and the GenerateSSHKey return it replaced. MaxStringBytes, MaxCausalReferences and the PayloadCeiling-bounded ParseEnvelope are intact.\n\nTo close: give the round-trip comparison a test that fails without it, using an embedded newline or CR rather than a padding mutation; add a test at each of the VerifySSHCommit and GenerateSSHKey boundaries; and either drop the keytype and empty-body guards as redundant or give them inputs the downstream parser would otherwise admit, so the names stop overstating what is covered.","body":{"branch":"task/sequencer-key-validation","head":"3d8604bc0d7702eb570840e92928c75664026d54","verdict":"CHANGES-REQUESTED"}}